<section class="container" style="padding-block:4rem 2rem;max-width:760px;">
  <p class="body-lg reveal" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">At Bruno Werner Schrankmöbel (“cabinet furniture”), questioning the company name itself was never on the table — it had simply always been there, like a family name. Lünstroth identified the name as the real obstacle to a contemporary presence that would be taken seriously internationally, and proposed “works”: the same name core already anchored in the market, reframed.</p>
  <p class="body-lg reveal reveal-delay-1" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">This proposal led to the complete execution: logo, word mark, corporate design and communication material — all rethought, starting from a question nobody had asked before.</p>
</section>

<section class="container" style="padding-block:1rem 3rem;max-width:760px;">
  <h2 class="display-md reveal" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">Why nobody questioned the name</h2>
  <p class="body-lg reveal" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">The vertical slats in the picture above are not decoration but company history. Bruno Werner Schrankmöbel became known in the 1960s for its vertical blind and its roll-front cabinets. The name carried a real success — and that is exactly what made it untouchable.</p>
  <p class="body-lg reveal reveal-delay-1" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">Here lies the pattern that reaches beyond this case: a name that once stood for a breakthrough is rarely checked for whether it can carry the next one. The greater the earlier success, the safer it is from the question. Forty years later, “Bruno Werner Schrankmöbel” described a range that no longer existed in that form and an ambition that no longer landed internationally — while its recall value in the market was still real.</p>
  <p class="body-lg reveal reveal-delay-2" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">That is why the name was not replaced but uncovered: “werner” stayed, because that is where the value lies. “Schrankmöbel” went, because that is where the limitation lay. It is the same logic as removing the symbol from the Gneuss logo — what the strategy demands is changed, and only that.</p>
</section>

